We are looking for volunteers who are willing to generate and maintain
ready-to-use packages for individual Linux distributions.

Future target: support a 386 with a 387 floating point coprocessor.
The current i386 runtime requires a Pentium because small allocations use
CMPXCHG8B. Replace that fast path with Storage.AllocateWithTag, check
allocation during startup and signal handling, and rebuild the runtime
and bootstrap compilers. Keep x87 floating point and HUGEINT DIV/MOD;
a processor without a floating point coprocessor is a separate target.

AMD64 runtime follow-up, before expanding the managed address space:
- Port SysIPC to the individual Linux shared-memory, message-queue and
  semaphore calls, with native kernel structures and 64-bit message types.
- Preserve full-width UNIXCALL results for address-valued results and
  recognize the complete Linux syscall error range.
- Extend regression coverage for signals, timers, coroutine stack growth
  and garbage collection under sustained allocation.
- Finally remove the low-2-GB allocator restriction, auditing address
  arithmetic, size types, type descriptors and the copying collector.

Smaller executables:
- Reduce the compulsory startup dependency graph, especially timezone,
  formatted I/O, persistence and networking support in minimal programs.
- Emit independently removable procedure sections and matching metadata;
  linker garbage collection currently cannot remove unused procedures
  from a retained module.
- Move zero-initialized globals to BSS while preserving their preceding
  garbage-collector tag, for example by initializing the tag at startup.
